  "answer": "The eradication of smallpox. In 1980 the WHO declared it gone — the first human disease ever deliberately wiped out, and one that had killed hundreds of millions of people over centuries.

What I admire is the ordinariness of how it was done. No single genius solved it. Thousands of health workers, many of them local, walked village to village with vaccines and a strategy of finding and containing every case. It required countries that distrusted each other to cooperate for decades. A virus that had shaped human history for millennia was cornered and eliminated by patient, unglamorous, collective effort.

That's the part that stays with me: humanity's best achievements are often not the dramatic ones, but the ones where a lot of people simply refused to give up on each other."
}

